WEBVTT

00:00:03.880 --> 00:00:18.560
'Every spring, our planet is transformed.'

00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:34.360
A riot of new life bursting from the ground.

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:44.840
'And it's all made possible by one rather misunderstood material.'

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:51.160
From early childhood we're told that this stuff, dirt, is best avoided.

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:56.520
But as someone with a lifelong passion for soil and everything that grows in it, it's a rule I've always enjoyed breaking.

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:04.520
'I'm Chris Beardshaw.

00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:08.520
I spend my life designing and planting gardens.

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:11.680
'Everything I do depends on soil.

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:17.960
'And I'm going to try and convince you that it's an unrecognised 'wonder of the natural world.'

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:27.960
For billions of years our land must've looked pretty much like this.

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:29.840
Bare rock.

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:30.600
A barren place.

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:33.920
Apparently devoid of life.

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:44.960
But something transformed it into a vibrant, living planet.

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:49.000
'And that something was soil.'

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:53.080
But what fascinates me is where did the soil come from?

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:56.760
What is it composed of and why is it so essential to life?

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:04.000
So I'm going to get down and dirty with soil.

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:09.080
I want to investigate its secrets.

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:12.520
And reveal it as you've never seen it before.

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:21.160
An intricate microscopic landscape... ..teeming with strange and wonderful life forms.

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:32.240
I'm going to reveal a world more complex and fragile than anything that exists above ground.

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:37.080
A substance so remarkable, you'll never walk on the grass in the same way again.

00:02:47.960 --> 00:02:54.520
'As a gardener, I spend my life among plants.

00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:03.520
'I see them emerge from the soil.

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:12.040
'But I've never really had the chance to discover what gives 'soil its amazing, life-giving force.

00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:18.680
'So now I want to find out.

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:23.280
'And I'm starting by doing what comes naturally.

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:25.080
'I'm going out to dig.'

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:39.120
Ask any gardener and they'll tell you that the soil provides their plants with the nutrients that are needed for life.

00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:50.120
And if you grow anything intensively, on farms or gardens, you have to apply fertiliser to replace and replenish those nutrients in the soil.

00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:57.800
In a natural landscape like this, all of these trees are being supported by the nutrients that are just inherently in the ground.

00:04:00.920 --> 00:04:06.520
But we shouldn't take these nutrients for granted.

00:04:05.720 --> 00:04:11.640
Like our fertilisers, they also need to be replenished.

00:04:10.760 --> 00:04:15.920
And how that happens is the first great mystery of soil.

00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:24.560
Even at the end of winter there's plenty of evidence of life on the woodland floor, or at least last season's life.

00:04:23.720 --> 00:04:28.720
Leaf litter, coming from the canopy above.

00:04:27.880 --> 00:04:33.880
But this is of no use at all to the surrounding plants in its current state.

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:47.000
That's because most plants simply can't feed on dead leaves and twigs.

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:50.600
They're too tough to break down and digest.

00:04:49.760 --> 00:04:51.160
And this creates a problem.

00:04:50.280 --> 00:05:01.440
Any nutrients they hold are locked in so the plants can't get at them.

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:03.400
'But hidden beneath the surface of the soil 'is a very different picture.'

00:05:03.320 --> 00:05:07.680
This modified-looking spade is actually a scientific instrument.

00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:18.880
The soil corer gives us the perfect cross section through the layers of the topsoil.

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:22.040
At the top we can see here this unrotted layer of leaf litter.

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:27.560
It's last season's leaves just sitting on the surface.

00:05:28.920 --> 00:05:37.520
But below that is a much darker layer where the particles are much more broken down, much smaller and quite compact.

00:05:36.680 --> 00:05:39.920
Beneath that is what we would recognise as topsoil.

00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:44.680
These are described by soil scientists as different horizons.

00:05:43.880 --> 00:05:48.160
'Collectively, the horizons are known as a soil profile.'

00:05:48.920 --> 00:05:58.720
And the deeper down the profile we go, the smaller the pieces of leaf and twig become until they just disappear.

00:05:57.960 --> 00:06:08.200
So somehow the tough plant matter is eventually broken down, releasing its trapped nutrients into the soil.

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:15.280
This is one of the most vital processes in nature.

00:06:15.080 --> 00:06:20.960
'And it's begun by a rather unlikely hero.

00:06:20.120 --> 00:06:29.920
'To help track it down, 'I'm joined by Lynne Boddy, Professor of Mycology 'at Cardiff University.

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:34.640
'We're on the hunt for an organism that prefers to stay 'out of the light.'

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:36.760
This is a likely-looking candidate, plenty of moss on the surface.

00:06:35.800 --> 00:06:37.680
Let's turn it over gently and see what we can see.

00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:43.920
Oh, it's wonderful, isn't it?

00:06:44.080 --> 00:06:47.800
Absolutely covered, it's almost like a spider's web under here, isn't it?

00:06:47.720 --> 00:06:49.680
It is.

00:06:48.880 --> 00:06:52.800
This is fungus.

00:06:51.920 --> 00:06:57.360
The crucial thing about the fungi is that they release nutrients which allow plants to continue to grow.

00:06:57.320 --> 00:07:07.320
The main body of the fungus is called the mycelium, which is made up of very, very, very fine filaments, they're too small to see by the naked eye.

00:07:06.480 --> 00:07:11.840
But here they're aggregated together to form cord- or root-like structures that we can clearly see.

00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:15.680
What do these threads do?

00:07:14.720 --> 00:07:26.520
They grow out from this wood in search of new resources, so maybe the resources would be dead leaves, more wood.

00:07:25.760 --> 00:07:33.280
When they find them they exude enzymes that break down the structure of the wood or the leaves or any other bits of dead plant material.

00:07:38.160 --> 00:07:41.840
It's easy to overlook fungi.

00:07:41.120 --> 00:07:45.560
But, to me, they're true champions of the natural world.

00:07:47.320 --> 00:07:58.680
They begin the process of breaking down dead wood and leaves to release the nutrients trapped inside.

00:07:59.080 --> 00:08:02.720
It's an extremely rare ability.

00:08:04.240 --> 00:08:22.800
The thing about the wood decay fungi is that actually they are the only organism or almost the only organism that can actually break down wood on this planet, and that is one of the reasons why they're so important, because otherwise we'd be up to our armpits in dead stuff.

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:32.640
And, in fact, plants wouldn't be able to grow because all the nutrients on this planet would be locked up in the dead plant material.

00:08:31.920 --> 00:08:42.280
As the fungus breaks down the leaves and twigs, it produces a rich substance we call humus that becomes part of the soil itself.

00:08:41.400 --> 00:08:47.000
But the fungus is doing another crucial job.

00:08:46.120 --> 00:08:53.960
It's feeding an entire world most of us don't even know exists.

00:08:53.280 --> 00:09:12.760
Using specialist microphotography, we can catch a rare glimpse of an astonishing hidden kingdom... ..teeming with weird, almost alien-looking life.

00:09:13.240 --> 00:09:25.480
Millions of tiny creatures, all of which are dependent on nutrients being released by the fungi.

00:09:24.600 --> 00:09:30.440
These are nematodes, tiny, round worms.

00:09:29.920 --> 00:09:38.120
Scientists think there may be up to half a million species of these wriggling in the soil.

00:09:42.040 --> 00:09:52.240
There are mites, tiny relatives of spiders and scorpions.

00:09:53.560 --> 00:10:01.520
Tardigrades, often called 'water bears' due to their cute appearance.

00:10:06.440 --> 00:10:18.200
And rotifers, fascinating little creatures that can propel themselves through the soil using special hairs that appear to revolve like a wheel.

00:10:25.720 --> 00:10:27.920
This is the first great secret of the soil.

00:10:27.080 --> 00:10:32.800
A vast, This is the first great secret of the soil.

00:10:32.000 --> 00:10:37.600
A vast, living kingdom of tiny animals.

00:10:36.760 --> 00:10:50.760
As they move around, eat and are in turn eaten themselves, they spread the essential nutrients released by the fungi.

00:10:49.960 --> 00:10:55.520
Helping to make the soil a more fertile place for growing plants.

00:11:04.440 --> 00:11:14.480
'Yet so far we've only seen how fungi 'begin the process of unlocking those nutrients.

00:11:13.720 --> 00:11:21.600
'Breaking down all the tough remains of dead plants is too large 'a job for fungi alone.

00:11:20.920 --> 00:11:25.640
'But they have a secret ally underground.

00:11:24.920 --> 00:11:31.640
'An animal whose impact on the soil is greater than any other.'

00:11:32.160 --> 00:11:38.760
When it comes to ecosystems, not all organisms are created equal.

00:11:37.880 --> 00:11:46.680
By that, what I mean is the work of one or two species will allow hundreds of others to thrive.

00:11:45.840 --> 00:11:54.480
One such animal is so important it's been called an ecosystem engineer.

00:11:53.600 --> 00:12:01.400
In this field, there might well be over two million of them.

00:12:02.840 --> 00:12:11.040
There are no prizes for guessing which animal I'm seeking out here.

00:12:10.880 --> 00:12:16.040
It's one that's inspired generations of horticulturists and agriculturists.

00:12:15.240 --> 00:12:17.960
It is possibly the greatest gardener on earth.

00:12:17.080 --> 00:12:21.720
And it's this, the humble earthworm.

00:12:25.200 --> 00:12:31.680
As a gardener, I've long known that worms play an important role in soil.

00:12:31.200 --> 00:12:46.600
The great Charles Darwin devoted over 40 years of study to them, culminating in the publication of his seminal work, The Formation Of Vegetable Mould Through The Actions Of Worms With Observations On Their Habits.

00:12:47.560 --> 00:12:59.440
You may not have heard of it, but it sold faster than On The Origin Of Species.

00:13:00.680 --> 00:13:09.480
Darwin's studies, lesser known than his work on evolution, revealed an organism that was essential for the life of the soil.

00:13:08.720 --> 00:13:10.560
He became obsessed by them.

00:13:09.960 --> 00:13:20.440
He fed them different diets, tested their intelligence and even tested their senses by playing a bassoon to them.

00:13:19.480 --> 00:13:24.400
What is about the earthworms that beguiled Darwin?

00:13:23.560 --> 00:13:26.520
Just why are they so important?

00:13:27.240 --> 00:13:34.920
Well, first of all the sheer scale of the worm operation.

00:13:34.360 --> 00:13:52.760
As they tunnel into the ground in their millions, their burrows permeate the earth like a vast ventilation system, providing essential supplies of air to everything else that lives in the soil.

00:13:53.360 --> 00:13:59.160
But that's not the earthworms' only talent.

00:13:58.440 --> 00:14:04.200
They also continue what the fungi began.

00:14:03.280 --> 00:14:10.840
They eat and digest dead leaves underground, unlocking their trapped nutrients.

00:14:10.880 --> 00:14:19.320
The way they do this reveals one of the most fundamental secrets of soil.

00:14:18.640 --> 00:14:22.560
But it's hard to see.

00:14:26.720 --> 00:14:37.760
'So I've come to meet Mark Hodson, Professor 'of Environmental Science at York University.'

00:14:36.800 --> 00:14:43.520
If you walk around after the rain you see them crawling around.

00:14:42.680 --> 00:14:49.000
'He's spent years studying what and how worms eat.'

00:14:48.760 --> 00:14:55.880
At night they come out onto the surface, they look round, sort of, sometimes they keep their tails anchored in their burrows.

00:14:55.080 --> 00:15:03.680
They sort of stretch out and eat or grab organic material, they pull it down into their burrows to eat later on.

00:15:02.800 --> 00:15:10.560
And the undigested material gets squirted out of the back end and that helps make all of this black, browny stuff which is the soil.

00:15:13.560 --> 00:15:21.480
Nothing is quicker at breaking down dead leaves than an earthworm.

00:15:20.800 --> 00:15:31.840
It's thought that in the average field the worms get through a staggering one and a half tonnes of plant matter every year.

00:15:30.920 --> 00:15:38.120
They're like leaf-processing factories, operating on an industrial scale.

00:15:37.440 --> 00:15:43.760
Yet they look nothing more than a simple, fleshy tube.

00:15:43.120 --> 00:15:46.800
So what's going on inside?

00:15:46.320 --> 00:15:52.440
To help answer that, Mark has been doing a rather unsavoury experiment.

00:15:52.040 --> 00:15:57.280
This Petri dish contains a sample of plain soil.

00:15:56.440 --> 00:16:05.360
And this one was made using earth that has passed through an earthworm.

00:16:05.520 --> 00:16:06.640
In other words, worm poo.

00:16:07.240 --> 00:16:17.240
Mark's been comparing the two and he's uncovered evidence of a hidden army of secret agents at work within the worm.

00:16:19.560 --> 00:16:21.560
Bacteria.

00:16:20.600 --> 00:16:24.400
So each of these spots is a bacterial colony.

00:16:23.600 --> 00:16:28.720
You can see there are far more growing here from the material that's just come out of the earthworm gut.

00:16:28.040 --> 00:16:38.320
So the earthworm ingests the soil, there are bacteria in there already, and the earthworm gut environment is good for bacteria.

00:16:37.520 --> 00:16:46.240
It's moist, its got the right pH, the earthworm is secreting mucous full of polysaccharide sugars, which the bacteria love to eat.

00:16:45.360 --> 00:16:55.360
So it's bacteria that finish the job of breaking down dead plant matter.

00:16:54.680 --> 00:17:07.319
There are billions of them naturally present in the ground, like workers on a production line turning dead plants into new soil.

00:17:06.760 --> 00:17:09.079
But inside the earthworm this activity is magnified to levels that are truly mind-blowing.

00:17:08.599 --> 00:17:14.160
This activity is magnified to levels that are truly mind-blowing.

00:17:17.240 --> 00:17:28.160
If you do counts on the soil in earthworm guts you can have 1,000 times more active bacteria in that soil than the bulk soil surrounding the earthworm.

00:17:27.359 --> 00:17:35.640
What it's proving is the earthworms have ramped up the bacterial activity in the soil.

00:17:36.520 --> 00:17:46.440
And it's this army of bacteria, hidden in the guts of earthworms, that completes the vital cycle.

00:17:45.520 --> 00:17:52.000
Unlocking all the nutrients from dead leaves and releasing them back into the soil.

00:17:54.840 --> 00:18:01.560
We very often think of soil as being brown, solid, inert stuff.

00:18:01.080 --> 00:18:09.520
But there's more life within in it than flies, swims or walks above it.

00:18:08.880 --> 00:18:22.160
And, far from being a haphazard array of organisms, this is a complex range of interconnected structures that support the life above.

00:18:27.440 --> 00:18:42.600
As we've seen, it takes a combination of plants, fungi, animals and bacteria all working together to keep nutrients flowing from the dead to the living.

00:18:41.720 --> 00:18:53.720
In the process, new soil is created which in turn supports even more life, making a cycle that keeps the soil fertile.

00:18:53.200 --> 00:19:00.920
Yet so far we've only scratched the surface of the soil.

00:19:00.040 --> 00:19:06.840
Everything we've seen happens within just the topmost layers.

00:19:12.200 --> 00:19:17.640
'Look deeper and there's far more to soil than this.

00:19:19.400 --> 00:19:24.520
'To reveal just how much, I first need a bit of heat.'

00:19:33.440 --> 00:19:39.120
What I have here is dried topsoil.

00:19:38.240 --> 00:19:45.360
I want to find out how much of this is derived from plants by setting fire to it.

00:19:44.560 --> 00:19:49.200
If it's 100% plant material, there should be nothing left.

00:19:48.440 --> 00:19:53.480
So I'm starting with 100g.

00:19:52.520 --> 00:19:56.680
'Let's see how much remains.'

00:20:07.360 --> 00:20:15.040
As this is burning away, the soil is completely transforming colour.

00:20:14.280 --> 00:20:17.320
It's going from a soft brown to almost a carbon colour.

00:20:16.360 --> 00:20:21.440
Very similar to the embers in a barbecue.

00:20:20.640 --> 00:20:24.880
The soil particles are fracturing, breaking apart.

00:20:24.560 --> 00:20:31.640
The organic matter binding them together is burning away and the soil particles are just falling to pieces.

00:20:32.160 --> 00:20:40.920
'The plant matter is turning into gases like carbon dioxide 'that are lost into the air.

00:20:40.320 --> 00:20:48.080
'After about 15 minutes of intense heat, I'm going to weigh it again.'

00:20:49.080 --> 00:20:53.920
We started off with about 100, it's now down to 70.

00:20:53.080 --> 00:20:57.080
So about 30% of this original soil was plant based.

00:20:56.120 --> 00:20:57.920
It's burnt away.

00:20:57.040 --> 00:21:01.800
Clearly, there's more to soil than just plant material.

00:21:07.160 --> 00:21:12.760
To see what that is, we need to get beneath the topsoil and look deeper down.

00:21:15.960 --> 00:21:27.640
'This is Scolly's Cross in Aberdeenshire, where 'a landslide has exposed the layers of soil beneath the pine forest.

00:21:26.840 --> 00:21:34.720
'It's something we rarely get to see, 'as all this is usually hidden underground.'

00:21:38.000 --> 00:21:52.440
In a landslip situation like this we get to examine perfectly the soil profile, the horizons or layers of various materials.

00:21:51.880 --> 00:22:03.080
At the top we've got the vegetation and, below, the various layers or horizons of soil, each with a different characteristic in terms of colours and textures.

00:22:02.600 --> 00:22:08.640
The topsoils, going down into the subsoils with the roots penetrating, this is what we saw in the forest.

00:22:11.880 --> 00:22:19.400
But, as we go further down, the dark organic plant material disappears.

00:22:18.560 --> 00:22:20.840
We seem to have left the soil behind.

00:22:23.000 --> 00:22:29.360
These deeper layers are mainly made up of fragments of the underlying rock.

00:22:29.760 --> 00:22:35.680
And then further down we're into bedrock.

00:22:34.720 --> 00:22:41.960
Collectively, these layers form the foundation of soil development.

00:22:47.600 --> 00:22:54.960
Rock fragments permeate the soil from the bedrock all the way to the surface.

00:22:55.120 --> 00:23:02.480
It's mainly this stuff that was left behind when I burned the plant matter away from the topsoil.

00:23:08.320 --> 00:23:16.160
But, though these particles are from lifeless rock, that doesn't mean they have no purpose.

00:23:15.520 --> 00:23:19.320
In fact, they are fundamental to how soil works.

00:23:29.120 --> 00:23:38.800
Soil particles are divided into three different categories depending on the size of the particle.

00:23:37.840 --> 00:23:40.920
The largest being sand.

00:23:40.000 --> 00:23:44.680
There you can see them just coming into focus, wonderful, rounded particles.

00:23:45.120 --> 00:23:49.600
The next size down, well, it's silt.

00:23:49.880 --> 00:23:56.520
And there you can start to see the individual silt particles.

00:23:55.720 --> 00:23:59.960
And the very smallest are the clays.

00:24:01.760 --> 00:24:18.320
Relatively speaking, if the sand was the size of a beach ball then the clay particles would be the size of a pin head.

00:24:17.560 --> 00:24:19.800
Incredibly small and flat in their profile.

00:24:18.960 --> 00:24:30.920
What's curious about the particles is that the relative proportions of them in any soil fundamentally affect how that soil behaves, and, more importantly, how it supports life.

00:24:43.320 --> 00:24:52.440
'To see exactly how, I've come to the James Hutton Institute 'in Aberdeen.

00:24:51.640 --> 00:24:57.280
'I'm here to meet soil scientist Dr Jason Owen.'

00:24:57.400 --> 00:25:00.160
Jason, what will this experiment demonstrate?

00:25:00.760 --> 00:25:02.760
What we have here are three cylinders.

00:25:01.920 --> 00:25:07.840
One with a sand, one with a silt-dominated soil and one with a clayed soil.

00:25:07.280 --> 00:25:12.440
When we pour water in the top what we'll see is the water percolating through the soil profile.

00:25:11.640 --> 00:25:15.240
With the sand it'll go very quickly.

00:25:17.760 --> 00:25:22.840
With a clay it'll go very slowly.

00:25:24.400 --> 00:25:27.320
And the silt will be somewhere in between.

00:25:29.280 --> 00:25:38.800
To me, this is familiar stuff, as it will be to any gardener.

00:25:38.000 --> 00:25:46.480
How well water moves through different types of soil.

00:25:46.480 --> 00:25:54.800
With the sand, large particles, there's quite large gaps, comparatively speaking, and water can go down through the profile.

00:25:54.040 --> 00:26:02.960
With the clay, very small particles, and as a result the gaps where water can penetrate are exceptionally small.

00:26:02.320 --> 00:26:06.080
The silt is somewhere in between the two extremes.

00:26:07.200 --> 00:26:13.440
But to really see what's going on inside the soil we have to look at it in far greater detail.

00:26:14.520 --> 00:26:21.560
Here, they're using cutting edge technology to examine soil on an incredibly small scale.

00:26:22.040 --> 00:26:31.520
We're joined by Evelyne Delbos, operator of the Scanning Electron Microscope at the Hutton Institute.

00:26:30.760 --> 00:26:38.000
She's looking at soil magnified 400 times.

00:26:38.600 --> 00:26:40.560
I have the three main parts of the soil.

00:26:39.680 --> 00:26:43.520
The sand grains here.

00:26:43.560 --> 00:26:46.480
On the right is the silt and the clay at the bottom.

00:26:47.400 --> 00:26:58.080
Well, you can sort of see with the clay, for example, it's stacked so tightly together that you can actually not see discernible gaps between them.

00:26:57.280 --> 00:27:06.760
Whereas here we've got these very large sand particles and even through they're right on top of each other you can still see the far larger gaps.

00:27:05.880 --> 00:27:11.920
That allows air, for aeration of the soil, and it also allows water movement through the soil.

00:27:13.240 --> 00:27:21.680
But there's more going on here than just how the particles are packed together.

00:27:21.000 --> 00:27:24.400
Let's imagine this is a grain of sand.

00:27:23.480 --> 00:27:31.840
And the surface area of that grain of sand is that surface, that surface, that surface, and that's it.

00:27:30.920 --> 00:27:47.440
It we take, by comparison, the same volume of clay then you have that surface plus that surface plus that surface, so you can imagine already that the surface area is much, much, much larger.

00:27:46.760 --> 00:27:50.920
So what does the surface area do to the water?

00:27:51.360 --> 00:27:52.840
What's the relationship between those two things?

00:27:52.280 --> 00:27:58.160
What's interesting about many clays, it has an electric charge associated with its surfaces.

00:27:57.400 --> 00:28:15.600
Many nutrients that are dissolved within the water can be attracted to these clay sites, to this large surface area, and then held, basically for root systems then to uptake for plant growth.

00:28:15.240 --> 00:28:26.840
So clay particles have an electrical charge that can bind nutrients and water to them.

00:28:26.040 --> 00:28:34.920
This allows soil to act as both larder and reservoir for plants and animals.

00:28:34.160 --> 00:28:35.800
Sounds ideal, but there's a catch.

00:28:34.960 --> 00:28:42.800
Too much clay and the soil can act like a sponge and can quickly become waterlogged.

00:28:41.920 --> 00:28:55.640
At the other end of the scale, too much sand and the water can run through too quickly, washing the nutrients out and leaving behind soil that's dry.

00:28:59.600 --> 00:29:02.600
Have we got an image of what a good soil should look like?

00:29:03.080 --> 00:29:10.520
Here you can see some grains of sand, they are different sizes.

00:29:09.760 --> 00:29:17.280
It's a mixture and you can also have there and there the clay and the silt all mixed up.

00:29:17.320 --> 00:29:20.680
So this is demonstrating the ideal, in terms of soil.

00:29:19.880 --> 00:29:28.080
It would be free draining, retain sufficient moisture, sufficient nutrients, what about microbial activity?

00:29:27.120 --> 00:29:44.840
This is a very, very complicated 3D structure which gives all of the microbiota within the soil effectively a niche, a home to live, and as a result the ecosystems that exist in the soil are exceptionally complicated.

00:29:44.080 --> 00:29:49.760
This is a classic example where you've got the mix between the large particles, the clay particles and silt all working together.

00:29:56.640 --> 00:30:04.240
So the elements that make up soil come from two very different places.

00:30:03.440 --> 00:30:08.920
The chaos of life, and the inert world of rock.

00:30:09.080 --> 00:30:18.720
Together, they create an intricate substance that can naturally feed and water all plant life on earth.

00:30:17.840 --> 00:30:27.480
And it makes me wonder just how did this strange alliance between rock and life begin?

00:30:31.720 --> 00:30:37.320
'How did the very first soil come to exist?'

00:30:40.960 --> 00:30:51.120
To find out, we need to go back to a time and place before the first soil appeared on the planet.

00:30:52.800 --> 00:30:57.800
That's not quite as difficult as it might sound.

00:30:57.960 --> 00:31:06.480
This is Malham Cove, an inland cliff deep in the Yorkshire Dales.

00:31:05.640 --> 00:31:17.000
It's a striking landscape, built from limestone and sculpted by the awesome power of ice.

00:31:23.160 --> 00:31:34.480
This place offers a wonderful window into the Earth billions of years ago, before there was soil.

00:31:33.720 --> 00:31:44.080
That's because at the end of the last Ice Age, as temperatures rose and the ice retreated, it left this naked rock.

00:31:43.680 --> 00:31:49.640
Any soil that had been here had been scoured away and deposited somewhere in that direction.

00:31:48.720 --> 00:31:54.080
And as a consequence any soil you see here is relatively new, in fact, it's still forming.

00:31:53.280 --> 00:31:56.400
Making this one of the best places in the country to discover how we get from this naked rock, to this.

00:31:55.440 --> 00:31:58.880
Soil that supports life.

00:32:05.520 --> 00:32:13.120
I'm joined by Professor Steven Nortcliff from Reading University.

00:32:13.560 --> 00:32:14.760
Landscape is fascinating in terms of the soil.

00:32:13.960 --> 00:32:20.720
First, I want to know what could possibly start to break up something as seemingly permanent as rock.

00:32:23.080 --> 00:32:26.400
We've got to break it down.

00:32:25.480 --> 00:32:33.080
And we've got evidence here in this landscape of those early stages of breakdown.

00:32:32.200 --> 00:32:41.440
We have ice forming in the fissures in the rock and as the ice expands it forces the rock apart.

00:32:40.960 --> 00:32:42.320
And that's the first form of disintegration.

00:32:41.360 --> 00:32:45.280
When water freezes, it expands.

00:32:45.440 --> 00:33:00.200
If that expansion happens within a crack, it can exert a force strong enough to break rock apart.

00:32:59.960 --> 00:33:02.680
And you can witness this in your own freezer at home.

00:33:01.880 --> 00:33:09.040
You fill the ice tray and when it freezes there's expansion.

00:33:08.200 --> 00:33:14.680
But it seems remarkable that that expansion is powerful enough to blow rock apart.

00:33:14.120 --> 00:33:17.640
Well, you're expanding in a confined space.

00:33:16.680 --> 00:33:18.720
It only has one way to expand and that's sideways.

00:33:17.920 --> 00:33:26.680
That forces the rock apart and it's the beginning of the disintegration to give us the soil.

00:33:31.160 --> 00:33:37.400
This process is called physical weathering.

00:33:36.520 --> 00:33:40.760
It breaks down rock by sheer brute force.

00:33:40.160 --> 00:33:46.640
But we're still a long way from soil.

00:33:46.320 --> 00:33:51.840
Next comes a different process entirely.

00:33:51.040 --> 00:33:53.120
And it starts with rain.

00:33:55.800 --> 00:34:01.560
We'll just drop some hydrochloric acid onto limestone.

00:34:00.600 --> 00:34:03.480
You can see it fizzing.

00:34:02.520 --> 00:34:03.560
You can hear it fizzing.

00:34:04.520 --> 00:34:10.199
It's really going at it.

00:34:09.320 --> 00:34:18.639
What Stephen's showing me is an exaggerated version of what happens every time it rains.

00:34:17.800 --> 00:34:29.000
Rain is slightly acidic and, with limestone, when this slightly acidic water falls on the surface it weathers it.

00:34:28.360 --> 00:34:30.360
And is that what we're seeing here, on the surface of the rock?

00:34:29.400 --> 00:34:33.520
That is exactly what we're seeing here.

00:34:38.159 --> 00:34:45.800
So rain reacts with the rock, gradually dissolving it.

00:34:45.080 --> 00:34:47.800
This is chemical weathering.

00:34:46.840 --> 00:34:52.239
The second key step towards soil.

00:34:53.639 --> 00:35:04.200
Using a stronger acid to speed the process up, we can see just how powerful it is.

00:35:03.320 --> 00:35:09.000
Here, a piece of rock is almost entirely dissolved.

00:35:08.240 --> 00:35:16.480
Leaving behind nothing but insoluble, sandy remains known as sediments.

00:35:15.680 --> 00:35:17.880
And that's the beginning of the soil.

00:35:17.400 --> 00:35:23.400
It's a very small amount of insoluble residue, but that's where the soil development starts.

00:35:29.120 --> 00:35:33.240
But sediment isn't yet soil.

00:35:32.560 --> 00:35:35.560
There's something fundamental missing.

00:35:36.880 --> 00:35:39.920
Life.

00:35:39.000 --> 00:35:46.520
But look closely, and this rock is not bare.

00:35:46.520 --> 00:35:51.320
It's covered in this, lichen.

00:35:50.720 --> 00:36:00.680
And this is what causes the final, almost magical metamorphosis from inert rock, to life-giving soil.

00:36:02.040 --> 00:36:15.600
In this environment they are key because the lichen will attack the rock, very much like the chemical weathering we saw, but it will break it down, release nutrients.

00:36:15.960 --> 00:36:24.560
Lichen is actually two organisms, algae and fungus, living in one body.

00:36:24.520 --> 00:36:39.360
And though it seems almost incredible, the fungus part is able to break down the rock to release nutrients that it can feed on.

00:36:38.480 --> 00:36:39.720
Much as we saw the fungi do with the wood in the forest.

00:36:39.680 --> 00:36:52.440
Over time, generations of lichen grow over one another, the new on top of the dead.

00:36:51.640 --> 00:36:55.280
The dead remains form organic matter.

00:36:54.400 --> 00:37:02.640
And when this mixes with sediment the result is soil.

00:37:02.680 --> 00:37:12.120
And so from an apparently barren limestone pavement up here we have the complete story of the generation of our soils.

00:37:11.160 --> 00:37:21.040
Bare rock through the various weathering processes, the biological processes and eventually the formation of soil.

00:37:20.120 --> 00:37:23.440
It is all here.

00:37:22.920 --> 00:37:24.280
Condensed into just a few square metres.

00:37:23.320 --> 00:37:28.720
Yeah, it's a wonderful example of soil development in motion.

00:37:27.760 --> 00:37:37.680
And what we've got is different areas representing different timescales - some it's just starting, others it's been going on for a few thousand years.

00:37:47.600 --> 00:38:00.480
Soil is the place where the relatively inert world of rock meets the riot of life above.

00:37:59.680 --> 00:38:22.440
It's a complex, staggeringly complex ecosystem, but it also offers something of a conundrum because the life creates soil, breaking down organic matter and forcing rocks apart, but that life is also dependent upon the soil for nutrients, moisture, habitat, anchorage, somewhere to live.

00:38:21.800 --> 00:38:30.720
That means there's a delicate balance between the life and the soil.

00:38:29.960 --> 00:38:34.480
Challenge one and you inevitably challenge the other.

00:38:40.440 --> 00:38:48.440
And today that ancient balance between rock and life is being challenged as never before in history.

00:38:52.200 --> 00:38:57.560
A new force has entered the world of the soil.

00:38:56.600 --> 00:38:57.960
Humankind.

00:39:02.920 --> 00:39:13.600
In geological terms, human civilisation is a mere blink of the eye, at around about 9,000 years.

00:39:12.760 --> 00:39:26.160
And in that brief moment in time we've arguably done more to change our soils than in the previous 400 million years.

00:39:26.200 --> 00:39:30.480
We've mined it.

00:39:29.760 --> 00:39:32.880
Built on it.

00:39:32.080 --> 00:39:36.480
Farmed on it.

00:39:35.720 --> 00:39:44.120
And, in places like this, drained it.

00:39:44.080 --> 00:39:50.960
And our actions have had consequences we never imagined.

00:39:51.360 --> 00:39:58.160
East Anglia is famed for its fenland landscape.

00:39:57.400 --> 00:40:00.680
One of rivers, marshes and streams.

00:39:59.960 --> 00:40:05.120
But what we have left is just a fraction of what was once here.

00:40:04.240 --> 00:40:16.920
Largely because this is a habitat that's prone to flooding and since the 17th century generation after generation have been progressively draining it.

00:40:17.520 --> 00:40:21.400
The great system of canals and ditches have been dug.

00:40:20.600 --> 00:40:25.400
To drain the unwanted water into the sea.

00:40:25.320 --> 00:40:31.280
Over the past 300 or so years, the population of the UK has grown rapidly.

00:40:30.400 --> 00:40:37.560
This put huge pressure on places like the fens.

00:40:36.760 --> 00:40:46.120
To help feed all those extra mouths, we needed to dry out the waterlogged land to make way for the business of agriculture.

00:40:45.400 --> 00:40:51.360
Rivers and lakes were drained and crops planted.

00:40:50.840 --> 00:40:53.000
The few people who lived there were thought rough and unfriendly.

00:40:52.040 --> 00:40:53.200
The few people who lived there were thought rough and unfriendly.

00:40:54.640 --> 00:41:03.280
Old ways of life and traditional pastimes that had grown up around the flooding were swept aside.

00:41:02.920 --> 00:41:05.200
But this progress came with a sting in the tail.

00:41:08.520 --> 00:41:15.040
As the rivers and meres were drained, something unexpected happened.

00:41:14.720 --> 00:41:16.120
The land began to sink.

00:41:18.920 --> 00:41:25.960
This is Holm Fen, drained in the 1850s.

00:41:25.080 --> 00:41:33.520
It was the home of Whittlesea Mere, once thought to be the second largest lake in England.

00:41:32.680 --> 00:41:34.880
This is all that's left.

00:41:38.720 --> 00:41:48.120
Previous experience had demonstrated that if you drain the fens the land would sink.

00:41:47.960 --> 00:41:53.880
So a local landowner here at Holme Fen, William Wells, decided to measure that process.

00:41:52.960 --> 00:42:00.360
He took a post and pushed it into the ground until the top was completely covered.

00:41:59.600 --> 00:42:00.680
And that post today?

00:41:59.800 --> 00:42:04.360
Well, here it is.

00:42:03.520 --> 00:42:06.640
The top of the post was originally ground level.

00:42:06.720 --> 00:42:18.240
Since 1850 this whole tract of land has sunk somewhere in the region of four metres, making this one of the lowest places in Britain.

00:42:17.520 --> 00:42:27.240
There can surely be no clearer indication of the effect of human interference on soil.

00:42:26.480 --> 00:42:27.560
But why did it sink?

00:42:26.760 --> 00:42:30.240
And what are the consequences?

00:42:32.400 --> 00:42:34.960
'I'm joined by Dr Ian Homan.

00:42:34.160 --> 00:42:41.200
'He and his colleagues at 'Cranfield University have extensively studied the area.

00:42:40.480 --> 00:42:47.440
'We're going to take a look at a rather special type of soil 'found here in the fens.

00:42:48.920 --> 00:42:51.600
'This is peat.'

00:42:50.680 --> 00:42:51.760
Pretty good profile.

00:42:53.920 --> 00:43:01.360
Peat forms in a wetland environment, so the soils are waterlogged.

00:43:00.640 --> 00:43:04.720
It's low in oxygen under the surface and it's quite acidic.

00:43:04.000 --> 00:43:13.840
So the combination of the waterlogged nature, the lack of oxygen and acidity slows down the rate of decomposition.

00:43:15.120 --> 00:43:24.440
The soil bacteria and the microbiological components of the soil aren't able to decompose that organic material.

00:43:23.480 --> 00:43:27.080
So it accumulates very slowly.

00:43:26.280 --> 00:43:34.680
So in peat, instead of being broken down, plant material builds up.

00:43:33.720 --> 00:43:37.480
And this has an important effect.

00:43:38.240 --> 00:43:48.120
Plants grow using carbon dioxide from the air.

00:43:47.480 --> 00:43:56.200
And if they're not broken down when they die they and the carbon they contain become trapped within the soil.

00:43:55.760 --> 00:44:03.240
This is what's known as a carbon sink and peat bogs are some of the best.

00:44:02.280 --> 00:44:11.000
But remove the water, and the balance changes.

00:44:10.200 --> 00:44:15.040
Oxygen enters the soil, allowing bacteria and fungi to breathe.

00:44:14.360 --> 00:44:20.920
This is what happened when the fens were drained and it had profound consequences.

00:44:24.040 --> 00:44:36.360
That allows the micro-organisms to use the carbon within this peat as an energy source, converting the carbon into carbon dioxide and energy.

00:44:35.480 --> 00:44:51.520
The fens, we think, are losing about four million cubic metres of peat soil every year and that equates to an emission of carbon dioxide of about 1, 1 million tonnes of carbon dioxide a year.

00:44:50.720 --> 00:45:01.560
We've gone from being an environment that should be storing carbon dioxide into the soil into an environment now that is emitting carbon dioxide.

00:45:00.720 --> 00:45:07.000
So the story of the fens really is that it's the worst possible, for both ends of the spectrum.

00:45:06.480 --> 00:45:11.040
Not only are we losing the carbon sink, but the carbon dioxide is being released into the atmosphere.

00:45:10.080 --> 00:45:11.960
Indeed.

00:45:16.480 --> 00:45:29.680
So as a result of human activity four metres of peat, which took thousands of years to form, disappeared in mere decades.

00:45:29.040 --> 00:45:43.480
And this old post is a monument to what can happen when we upset the balance within the soils.

00:45:43.920 --> 00:45:48.960
It's a story that's repeated throughout human history.

00:45:48.440 --> 00:46:01.800
Archaeological records very clearly demonstrate that, as our nomadic ancestors began to settle and farm the land, populations increased dramatically.

00:46:00.840 --> 00:46:10.760
And in order to feed the population the area of land that was turned over to the plough also increased.

00:46:10.200 --> 00:46:20.400
Those early farmers tilled and ploughed, fertilised and irrigated in the best way they knew how.

00:46:19.440 --> 00:46:26.080
But, as we've seen, human interference can have unexpected consequences.

00:46:28.920 --> 00:46:34.680
Ploughing and tilling can destroy the soil's structure.

00:46:33.720 --> 00:46:45.760
Intensive farming will deplete the soil of nutrients and over-irrigation can cause high levels of toxicity.

00:46:45.480 --> 00:46:55.080
When these factors combine the soil becomes degraded and prone to erosion from wind and water.

00:46:55.240 --> 00:47:02.360
For me, recent history provides a stark warning.

00:47:01.520 --> 00:47:11.760
By the 1930s, vast swathes of the North American prairies were turned over to the plough.

00:47:11.080 --> 00:47:18.280
All the way from Canada down to Texas.

00:47:17.480 --> 00:47:22.000
But this would lead to catastrophe.

00:47:22.480 --> 00:47:26.960
High winds and sun.

00:47:26.160 --> 00:47:32.680
A country without rivers and with little rain.

00:47:32.840 --> 00:47:43.680
Intensive farming techniques had weakened the structure of the soil till it could no longer hold itself together.

00:47:42.840 --> 00:47:49.680
So when a drought came the soil dried out then simply blew away.

00:47:48.840 --> 00:47:52.680
Turning the prairies into a huge dustbowl.

00:47:51.720 --> 00:47:56.320
The rains failed and the sun baked the light soil.

00:47:58.200 --> 00:48:03.000
It affected 100,000,000 acres of land.

00:48:02.440 --> 00:48:11.800
By 1940, over 2 million people had been forced off the prairies.

00:48:12.360 --> 00:48:13.760
Their stock choked to death on the barren land.

00:48:12.880 --> 00:48:20.160
Their homes nightmares of swirling dust night and day.

00:48:19.440 --> 00:48:22.520
Many went to heaven.

00:48:21.880 --> 00:48:28.600
It was one of the biggest environmental disasters in American history.

00:48:29.560 --> 00:48:34.600
But today the problem is potentially worse than it ever was.

00:48:37.040 --> 00:48:44.520
There are now more than seven billion human beings on the planet.

00:48:44.040 --> 00:48:53.560
There are more of us alive today than there have been up to the 20th century.

00:48:52.920 --> 00:48:59.040
So it comes as no surprise more is being taken from the soil.

00:48:58.160 --> 00:49:00.440
We're more reliant on the soil than ever before.

00:48:59.640 --> 00:49:08.600
In trying to satisfy that need we're cultivating, tilling, fertilising to keep our soil productive.

00:49:07.800 --> 00:49:14.280
In doing so, we're destroying the delicate structural balance of the soil.

00:49:14.400 --> 00:49:26.040
So when we talk about an impending food crisis what we should actually be talking about is a soil crisis.

00:49:26.400 --> 00:49:35.400
And that crisis is being felt as keenly in the UK as anywhere else.

00:49:34.560 --> 00:49:40.360
It's brought this farm in Ross-on-Wye to the brink of ruin.

00:49:44.320 --> 00:49:54.240
Asparagus farmer John Chinn has seen massive gullies open up in his fields.

00:49:53.400 --> 00:50:07.880
Weakened by farming, the soil was washed away by the rain, taking his crop with it.

00:50:07.400 --> 00:50:13.360
So what is it about the conventional way of managing a crop like asparagus that was causing that degree of erosion?

00:50:12.680 --> 00:50:16.240
It's two sides.

00:50:15.560 --> 00:50:22.120
The first is that we have soil exposed the whole time.

00:50:21.160 --> 00:50:30.280
Then, secondly, because we didn't want water standing in the crop we would plant the rows up and down the slope so the water would run off.

00:50:29.360 --> 00:50:39.920
Of course, what was happening was that the water was running off faster and faster and as it went it picked up the soil because it was just there on the surface.

00:50:39.080 --> 00:50:52.400
Carried that soil out to the bottom of the field, maybe into a stream, a road, leaving behind it a gully that as you came down the slope got deeper and deeper.

00:50:52.120 --> 00:50:55.880
We have an amber warning in force for the Somerset Levels.

00:50:58.080 --> 00:51:05.080
Water erosion has become a devastating problem in the UK.

00:51:05.560 --> 00:51:09.600
Could be another 20mm or perhaps a bit more in this area.

00:51:09.320 --> 00:51:19.040
Over the past five years, we've experienced an unusually high number of storms, culminating in the winter of 2013.

00:51:22.040 --> 00:51:27.960
It was the wettest on record.

00:51:27.120 --> 00:51:39.120
Vast swathes of the UK suffered rainfall on an almost biblical scale, leaving many areas like the Somerset Levels deluged for months.

00:51:39.680 --> 00:51:50.280
It's this kind of rainfall that was partly to blame for the destruction of John's asparagus fields.

00:51:49.720 --> 00:51:59.240
In desperation, he sought the advice of soil specialists at Cranfield University.

00:51:59.200 --> 00:52:03.480
One of them was Dr Rob Simmons.

00:52:02.640 --> 00:52:10.400
'He's investigating the huge problem of water erosion on the smallest 'possible scale.

00:52:09.840 --> 00:52:17.080
'By studying the energy within individual raindrops.'

00:52:16.840 --> 00:52:23.560
The raindrop has a certain mass and a velocity which affects its kinetic energy.

00:52:23.120 --> 00:52:34.520
When that raindrop with that kinetic energy impacts on the soil surface it will damage the soil and cause breakdown at the soil surface.

00:52:34.680 --> 00:52:44.440
As you start to get extreme rainfall events you get short-duration, high-energy events with a larger drop size, more kinetic energy and they're going to cause more damage to your soil surface.

00:52:43.600 --> 00:52:46.480
And it's those that we're having more of?

00:52:45.840 --> 00:52:48.280
And it's those that we're having more of.

00:52:48.280 --> 00:52:49.840
Yep.

00:52:48.880 --> 00:52:55.720
Rob is testing what happens when rain hits soil.

00:52:55.280 --> 00:53:02.680
It's immediately apparent that excess water quickly starts to flow across the surface, what the scientists call run-off.

00:53:03.840 --> 00:53:09.760
Right, what we can see here is that run-off is being generated almost straight away.

00:53:09.000 --> 00:53:16.840
So expanded out onto a large field situation this could cause major problems.

00:53:15.960 --> 00:53:18.600
This is all well and good in a lab, but is there anything you can do about it out in the field?

00:53:17.840 --> 00:53:20.440
Absolutely, but the best thing to do is to go out in the fields.

00:53:19.560 --> 00:53:22.000
Where the sun is shining.

00:53:22.400 --> 00:53:37.800
By understanding exactly what happens when raindrops hit soil, Rob has been able to help John make some big changes to the way he farms.

00:53:37.680 --> 00:53:40.240
And they're surprisingly low-tech.

00:53:39.560 --> 00:53:47.920
Instead of planting straight up and down the hillside, John now plants his rows on the diagonal.

00:53:47.320 --> 00:53:50.160
And he plants grass strips between them.

00:53:50.160 --> 00:53:59.240
The combined effect is to slow down the run-off of water, reducing its power to erode the soil.

00:53:58.360 --> 00:54:00.120
But that's only the beginning.

00:53:59.440 --> 00:54:08.800
Now Rob's come up with an ingenious new idea to take the energy 'out of the rain itself.

00:54:08.000 --> 00:54:16.080
'To test it, he's set up rainfall simulators 'and dug a series of channels, or wheelings.'

00:54:17.080 --> 00:54:20.920
We've got two rainfall simulators.

00:54:20.120 --> 00:54:24.280
We've got a wheeling which is bare on the left-hand side.

00:54:23.640 --> 00:54:26.560
And on the right-hand side we've got a wheeling which has got straw mulch in it.

00:54:25.720 --> 00:54:32.360
What the straw will do is it will absorb the energy of that rainfall.

00:54:31.400 --> 00:54:38.200
It will also act as a blanket effectively and it will absorb some of that water, slow down the run-off.

00:54:38.280 --> 00:54:44.880
It seems an incredibly simple solution, basic straw.

00:54:53.880 --> 00:54:56.920
Comparing the two scenarios side by side reveals a big difference.

00:55:02.960 --> 00:55:09.880
Raindrops hit the bare earth with force and break up the soil.

00:55:09.120 --> 00:55:16.360
Run-off water soon begins to flow and carry the soil away.

00:55:15.560 --> 00:55:21.760
But here the large drops are broken up before they can hit the ground.

00:55:21.400 --> 00:55:27.720
It's the straw, not the soil, that takes the brunt of the impact.

00:55:27.720 --> 00:55:32.320
And the run-off is reduced to a trickle.

00:55:32.960 --> 00:55:43.080
By having that canopy it absorbs all the energy, you don't have the detachment, you don't have the run-off and erosion problems.

00:55:44.800 --> 00:55:50.800
What's your reaction to the technology which is now being deployed in the field?

00:55:51.000 --> 00:56:00.680
Well, I suppose as a farmer it started off as scepticism, you know, here's a chap from the university.

00:55:59.840 --> 00:56:06.280
Yes, he can solve civil engineering problems, mining quarrying problems, but this is farming.

00:56:05.360 --> 00:56:11.920
And so it's taken a little while, I think, hasn't it, Rob?

00:56:11.080 --> 00:56:15.160
You've worked on me, you've shown me that it works.

00:56:14.400 --> 00:56:18.320
Now that's starting to snowball.

00:56:17.520 --> 00:56:28.920
That's going out to other farmers and I think that in 10 years' time the sort of things were doing now will become standard practice and frankly to not do them will become unacceptable.

00:56:28.160 --> 00:56:31.640
We have to look after the soil, it's a valuable resource.

00:56:36.560 --> 00:56:47.640
To me, it's astonishing that a potentially huge threat to soil can be averted using something as low-tech as straw.

00:56:46.880 --> 00:56:53.400
All it needs is a little thought and a willingness to change.

00:56:52.800 --> 00:57:03.160
I believe these are vital if we're to avoid the mistakes of our past and preserve this most precious of resources.

00:57:02.560 --> 00:57:11.480
And research like this and the commitment of farmers like John give me hope that we'll achieve that.

00:57:18.480 --> 00:57:31.120
So, whilst we have a chequered history when it comes to our relationship with soil, it does seem at last we're beginning to understand and appreciate what an amazing substance it is.

00:57:32.520 --> 00:57:40.040
'Exploring soil, we've uncovered the secrets of its life-giving force.'

00:57:39.080 --> 00:57:49.120
We've revealed an intricate living system, where life meets rock at the microscopic scale.

00:57:50.440 --> 00:58:02.840
Each acting on the other in complex and surprising ways to form what to me is, without doubt, the most fascinating and important material on the face of the planet.

00:58:02.360 --> 00:58:08.680
So the next time you walk on the grass give a nod of thanks to the hidden rainforest beneath your feet.
